The '''2012 NBA draft''' was held on June 28, 2012, at [[Prudential Center]] in [[Newark, New Jersey]]. The draft started at 7:00&nbsp;pm [[Eastern Time Zone (North America)|Eastern Daylight Time]] (2300 [[Coordinated Universal Time|UTC]]), and was broadcast in the United States on [[ESPN]]. In this [[Draft (sports)|draft]], [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA) teams took turns selecting amateur U.S. [[college basketball]] players and other eligible players, including international players. The [[New Orleans Hornets]], who had a 13.70 percent probability of obtaining the first selection, won the [[NBA draft lottery]] on May 30. The [[Charlotte Bobcats]] and the [[Washington Wizards]] were second and third, respectively. This draft marked the first time that the first two players selected were from the same school ([[Anthony Davis]] and [[Michael Kidd-Gilchrist]] were teammates at [[Kentucky Wildcats men's basketball|Kentucky]]).<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hoffman |first=Benjamin |date=June 28, 2012 |title=Kentucky Reigns Again on N.B.A.'s Draft Night |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/sports/basketball/hornets-take-anthony-davis-with-top-pick-in-draft.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120702092226/http://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/sports/basketball/hornets-take-anthony-davis-with-top-pick-in-draft.html |archive-date=July 2, 2012 |access-date=February 26, 2017 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> It also set a record of having six players from one school (Kentucky) being selected in the two rounds of the draft and was the first draft to have the first three selections be college freshmen all from the same conference ([[Southeastern Conference]]). [[Bernard James]] was the oldest player drafted in an NBA draft, being 27 years old at the time of the draft.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beltran |first=Jess Matthew |date=July 29, 2012 |title=Bernard James: The 27-Year-Old Rookie |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/1276690-bernard-james-the-27-year-old-rookie |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181017001911/https://bleacherreport.com/articles/1276690-bernard-james-the-27-year-old-rookie |archive-date=October 17, 2018 |access-date=May 26, 2023 |website=[[Bleacher Report]]}}</ref>

The draft contained seven players who combined for 10 championships as well as having Anthony Davis and [[Damian Lillard]] both named to the [[NBA 75th Anniversary Team]] in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 21, 2021 |title=NBA 75th Anniversary Team announced |url=https://www.nba.com/news/nba-75th-anniversary-team-announced |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211022003548/https://www.nba.com/news/nba-75th-anniversary-team-announced |archive-date=October 22, 2021 |access-date=June 26, 2023 |website=[[NBA.com]]}}</ref> Six players have participated in an [[NBA All-Star Game|All-Star Game]]. [[Draymond Green]] won [[NBA championship]]s with the [[Golden State Warriors]] in [[2015 NBA Finals|2015]], [[2017 NBA Finals|2017]], [[2018 NBA Finals|2018]], and [[2022 NBA Finals|2022]], as well as being named to the [[All-NBA Team|All-NBA]] second team in 2016 and the third team in 2017. Green was named the [[NBA Defensive Player of the Year Award|Defensive Player of the Year]] in [[2016–17 NBA season|2017]], while also [[List of National Basketball Association annual steals leaders|leading the league in steals]] that season. He was named to the [[NBA All-Defensive Team|All-Defensive]] first team in [[2014–15 NBA season|2015]]–2017, [[2020–21 NBA season|2021]], and [[2024–25 NBA season|2025]], and named to the second team in [[2017–18 NBA season|2018]]–[[2018–19 NBA season|2019]] and [[2021–22 NBA season|2022]]–[[2022–23 NBA season|2023]]. He was an All-Star in [[2016 NBA All-Star Game|2016]]–[[2018 NBA All-Star Game|2018]] and [[2022 NBA All-Star Game|2022]].<ref name="DraymondGreenStats">{{Cite web |title=Draymond Green NBA Statistics |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/g/greendr01.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016060747/https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/g/greendr01.html |archive-date=October 16, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[Basketball-Reference.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cato |first=Tim |date=June 26, 2017 |title=Draymond Green is finally the Defensive Player of the Year award winner after 2 runners-up |url=https://www.sbnation.com/2017/6/26/15871398/2017-nba-awards-defensive-player-of-the-year-draymond-green |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170907002442/https://www.sbnation.com/2017/6/26/15871398/2017-nba-awards-defensive-player-of-the-year-draymond-green |archive-date=September 7, 2017 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[SB Nation]]}}</ref> Anthony Davis won a championship with the [[Los Angeles Lakers]] in [[2020 NBA Finals|2020]] and was All-NBA 5 times; first team in 2015, 2017–2018, and [[2019–20 NBA season|2020]], and second team in [[2023–24 NBA season|2024]]. Davis was named to the All-Defensive first team in 2018, 2020, and 2024, and second team in 2015 and 2017. He was the [[List of National Basketball Association annual blocks leaders|NBA blocks leader]] in [[2013–14 NBA season|2014]], 2015, and 2018. Davis was an All-Star from [[2014 NBA All-Star Game|2014]]–[[2021 NBA All-Star Game|2021]], and [[2024 NBA All-Star Game|2024]]–[[2025 NBA All-Star Game|2025]], as well as the [[NBA All Star Game MVP]] in [[2017 NBA All-Star Game|2017]].<ref name="AnthonyDavisStats">{{Cite web |title=Anthony Davis NBA Statistics |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/d/davisan02.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016162534/https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/d/davisan02.html |archive-date=October 16, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[Basketball-Reference.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=NBA Season Blocks Leaders |url=https://www.nba.com/stats/leaders?StatCategory=BLK |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230925111907/https://www.nba.com/stats/leaders?StatCategory=BLK |archive-date=September 25, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[NBA.com]]}}</ref> Damian Lillard became the fourth player to be unanimously named [[NBA Rookie of the Year Award|Rookie of the Year]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=<!--SLAM Staff--> |date=May 2, 2013 |title=Damian Lillard the Fourth Unanimous NBA Rookie of the Year |url=https://www.slamonline.com/archives/damian-lillard-the-fourth-unanimous-nba-rookie-of-the-year/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211204084526/https://www.slamonline.com/archives/damian-lillard-the-fourth-unanimous-nba-rookie-of-the-year/ |archive-date=December 4, 2021 |access-date=May 26, 2023 |magazine=[[SLAM Magazine]]}}</ref> Lillard was a member of the All-NBA first team in 2018, second team in 2016, 2019–2021, and third team in 2014, 2023. He was an All-Star in 2014, [[2015 NBA All-Star Game|2015]], 2018–2021, and [[2023 NBA All-Star Game|2023]]–2025, the All-Star Game MVP in 2024, as well as the winner of the [[Three-Point Contest]] in 2023, 2024, and [[2026 NBA All-Star Game|2026]].<ref name="DamianLillardStats">{{Cite web |title=Damian Lillard NBA Statistics |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/l/lillada01.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231008232234/https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/l/lillada01.html |archive-date=October 8, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[Basketball-Reference.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bontemps |first=Tim |date=February 18, 2023 |title=Lillard secures 'a goal of mine,' wins NBA 3-point contest |url=https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/35692201/lillard-secures-goal-wins-nba-3-point-contest |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230219043448/https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/35692201/lillard-secures-goal-wins-nba-3-point-contest |archive-date=February 19, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[ESPN]]}}</ref><ref name="Lillard2024AllStarAwards">{{Cite web |last=Nehm |first=Eric |date=February 19, 2024 |title=Damian Lillard turns All-Star Weekend into Dame Time with game MVP, 3-Point Contest win |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5284794/2024/02/19/damian-lillard-turns-all-star-weekend-into-dame-time-with-game-mvp-3-point-contest-win/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240219140751/https://theathletic.com/5284794/2024/02/19/damian-lillard-turns-all-star-weekend-into-dame-time-with-game-mvp-3-point-contest-win/ |archive-date=February 19, 2024 |access-date=February 24, 2024 |website=[[The Athletic]]}}</ref> [[Khris Middleton]] won a championship with the [[Milwaukee Bucks]] in [[2021 NBA Finals|2021]] and was selected as an All-Star in [[2019 NBA All-Star Game|2019]]–[[2020 NBA All-Star Game|2020]], and 2022.<ref name="KhrisMiddletonStats">{{Cite web |title=Khris Middleton NBA Statistics |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/m/middlkh01.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230920035133/https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/m/middlkh01.html |archive-date=September 20, 2023 |access-date=October 20, 2023 |website=[[Basketball-Reference.com]]}}</ref> As of 2025, Davis, [[Bradley Beal]], Lillard, [[Harrison Barnes]], [[Andre Drummond]], Green, and Middleton are the only players from this draft who are still playing in the NBA.

Of the players drafted, 30 were [[Forward (basketball)|forwards]], 21 were [[Guard (basketball)|guards]], and 9 were [[Center (basketball)|centers]]. The draft marked the first appearance of the [[Brooklyn Nets]] and the last draft appearance for the New Orleans Hornets. After the [[2012–13 NBA season|2012–13 season]], the franchise was renamed as the [[New Orleans Pelicans]]. New Orleans made their first draft appearance as the Pelicans in [[2013 NBA draft|2013]]. Four of the players selected in this draft never played in an NBA game throughout their professional basketball careers. Two of those players were the sole selection of the draft by their respective teams: [[İlkan Karaman]] (Brooklyn's only pick) and [[Marcus Denmon]] (San Antonio's only pick).

